think Studied the two halves the rung joins: image/qoi.rye (HUNK0 — encode/decode, Pixmap, encoded_bound, six-op round-trip) and pond/apps/tablecloth.rye (store_artifact beads content under a name; fetch_artifact reassembles, proving every bead against its digest and the whole against its recorded address → DigestMismatch on tamper). The tablecloth family and its symlink infrastructure (mantra/beading.rye, parse_int.rye, tally_copy.rye) all live in pond/apps/, so the bridge module belongs there; symlinked qoi.rye into pond/apps/ (code untouched).
think Bounds: beading.max_resin_bytes = 512, so max_content_bytes = 512 — a stored QOI stream must fit a resin. The crafted 8×8 image encodes to 166 bytes over 2 beads, well within the bound. Named max_stream_bytes = tablecloth.max_content_bytes and refused StreamTooLarge before storing anything a fetch could not hold.
think Wrote pond/apps/image_artifact.rye — store_image (encode → store_artifact) and fetch_image (fetch_artifact → decode). The crux ordering is load-bearing: fetch_artifact's digest check runs and returns FIRST; only on Ok does qoi.decode run, so a tampered bead refuses before a single pixel is read. Selftest rides a real image through the store byte-for-byte, proves the dedup dividend (same image, two names, stored once), and proves the tampered-bead (DigestMismatch) and unknown-name (UnknownArtifact) refusals both land before decode.
